infographic compare-binary-horizontal-underline-text-vs
data
  title Two-token design: access token vs refresh token
  desc Two credentials, deliberately opposite — one short and stateless, one long and trackable
  compares
    - label Access token (short-lived)
      children
        - label TTL ~15 minutes
          desc A short exp caps a stolen token to a 15-minute problem, not forever
        - label Stateless, self-contained JWT
          desc Verified by signature with no DB lookup — which is why it scales
        - label Cannot be revoked before exp
          desc No server-side record to delete; the TTL is the only lever
        - label Lives in memory
          desc Attached to each request to authorize it
    - label Refresh token (long-lived)
      children
        - label TTL 7-30 days
          desc Long enough to avoid constant re-login; used only at /auth/refresh
        - label Server-side trackable
          desc A family record tracks the live token and whether it is revoked
        - label Rotates single-use, revocable
          desc Each refresh mints a new token; a reused one kills the whole family
        - label httpOnly cookie, stored hashed
          desc Secure SameSite cookie hides it from JS; the DB holds only a SHA-256 hash
theme
  palette #6366f1 #14b8a6
